1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,729
How can one light push of your foot stop a car that weighs over a ton?

2
00:00:04,899 --> 00:00:10,349
The secret: a liquid you can't squeeze carries your push to the wheels.

3
00:00:10,519 --> 00:00:15,689
Push on a fluid in a closed pipe: the pressure is equal everywhere.

4
00:00:15,859 --> 00:00:20,078
The pedal is a lever, and a booster adds even more force.

5
00:00:20,248 --> 00:00:25,047
It pushes a small piston in the master cylinder, pressurizing the fluid.

6
00:00:25,217 --> 00:00:28,344
Brake lines carry that pressure to each wheel.

7
00:00:28,514 --> 00:00:33,057
The caliper's piston is much wider, so the force gets multiplied.

8
00:00:33,227 --> 00:00:38,363
The caliper squeezes two pads onto the spinning disc, like pinching fingers.

9
00:00:38,533 --> 00:00:43,204
The disc is bolted to the wheel, so gripping it slows the whole car.

10
00:00:43,374 --> 00:00:49,172
Friction turns motion into heat, about 130 degrees Celsius in normal braking.

11
00:00:49,342 --> 00:00:53,619
Many discs have inner fins, like a fan, to shed that heat.

12
00:00:53,789 --> 00:00:58,367
Surprise: disc brakes helped Jaguar win Le Mans in 1953!

13
00:00:58,537 --> 00:01:02,233
Lift your foot, the pressure drops, and the pads let go.

14
00:01:02,403 --> 00:01:06,587
So: fluid multiplies your push, friction turns speed into heat.

15
00:01:06,757 --> 00:01:11,812
Tech tip: brake fluid soaks up water, so change it about every two years.

16
00:01:11,982 --> 00:01:17,683
Cogwise: how things work. Follow for the next one!
